// Does `map_get_hex` hand back a MUTABLE VIEW of the stored cell, or a copy?
//
// This is not curiosity — the answer decides what the write guard has to be
// (plan 8, Guard 3). loft's writing guide warns that `tmp = v[j]` on a
// `vector<STRUCT>` is a VIEW of slot j and not a copy (#338), and `map_get_hex`
// returns `gh_c.ck_hexes[idx]` — a vector element. If that view survived the
// return, every `cur = map_get_hex(…); cur.h_field = …` in the tree would already
// have written the map, the trailing `map_set_hex` would be decorative, and ANY
// caller could mutate a cell through no function at all.
//
// MEASURED: it copies. The map is unchanged until `map_set_hex` runs.
//
// So the chokepoint is real rather than illusory — and the remaining problem is
// the opposite one: seven sites each independently REMEMBER to call it, and
// forgetting is silent (the edit is simply lost). Guard 3 is therefore "route the
// seven through one column-taking write", not "stop handing out views".
//
// ⚠ Pinned as a gate because the design depends on it and it may not be stable:
// `Hex` became eight bytes of narrow integers in the compact-voxel migration, and
// a small value may well copy where the old 56-byte record aliased. If loft's
// semantics move, this fails and Guard 3 has to be rethought — which is exactly
// the notice we would want.
use moros_map;

fn test_the_getter_returns_a_copy_not_a_view() {
  m = map_empty();
  map_set_height(m, 3, 4, 0, 100);

  cur = map_get_hex(m, 3, 4, 0);
  cur.h_height = 999;                    // mutate ONLY the returned value
  // deliberately no map_set_hex

  back = map_get_hex(m, 3, 4, 0);
  // This assertion is its own control: reading 100 proves the store-and-read path
  // works, so a pass cannot mean "the cell was never written in the first place".
  assert(back.h_height == 100,
         "the getter ALIASES — the map became {back.h_height} with no map_set_hex. "
         + "Guard 3 must stop handing out mutable views, not merely add a chokepoint");
}
